                sections: [
                    AwakeningSection(heading: "The Shocking Discovery", body: "For decades, cognitive science assumed human thinking follows classical logic. Yes/no. True/false. If A then B. Boolean operators. Clean, predictable, computable.\n\nThere was just one problem: humans kept breaking the rules.\n\nPeople consistently violate classical probability in their judgments. They commit the conjunction fallacy (thinking two events together are MORE probable than either alone). Their decisions change depending on the ORDER questions are asked. They show interference effects — where considering option A changes the probability of choosing option B, even when A and B are logically independent.\n\nClassical probability theory can't explain this. For decades, these were called \"cognitive biases\" — bugs in human reasoning that evolution should have fixed.\n\nThen Busemeyer, Bruza, and others asked a radical question: what if human cognition isn't buggy? What if it's just not CLASSICAL?\n\nStudy: Busemeyer & Bruza (2012, 2nd ed. 2024) — Quantum Models of Cognition and Decision, Cambridge University Press.\n\nThey applied quantum probability theory — the same math that describes subatomic particles — to human judgment. And it fit. Better than any classical model.\n\nNot because the brain is a quantum computer (that's a different debate). But because the MATHEMATICAL STRUCTURE of quantum probability — superposition, interference, contextuality, non-commutativity — describes how human minds actually process uncertainty better than classical Boolean logic does."),
                    AwakeningSection(heading: "What This Means in Plain English", body: "SUPERPOSITION\nIn quantum physics, a particle can be in multiple states simultaneously until measured. In your mind: you can hold contradictory beliefs, feelings, and possibilities at the same time — and they're all \"real\" until a decision collapses them into one.\n\nEver felt simultaneously excited AND terrified about the same thing? That's not confusion. That's superposition. Both states are real. The \"measurement\" (decision or action) collapses them into one experience.\n\nINTERFERENCE\nIn quantum physics, probability waves can amplify or cancel each other. In your mind: thinking about one option genuinely changes the probability of choosing another. Creativity often works this way — two unrelated ideas interfere constructively and produce an insight that neither could have produced alone.\n\nThis is why brainstorming works. Why meditation produces creative breakthroughs. Why the best ideas come in the shower. You're allowing interference between mental states that focused \"classical\" thinking keeps separate.\n\nNON-COMMUTATIVITY\nIn quantum physics, the order of measurements matters — measuring position then momentum gives different results than momentum then position. In your mind: the order in which you consider information changes your judgment.\n\nStudy: Order effects in surveys. Ask \"Is the president trustworthy?\" then \"Is the president competent?\" versus the reverse order. You get statistically different results. Classical probability says order shouldn't matter. Quantum probability predicts exactly this.\n\nCONTEXTUALITY\nIn quantum physics, the outcome depends on how you measure. In your mind: the same information produces different judgments depending on context. Your shadow material creates context — unprocessed fear changes how you interpret neutral data.\n\nThis is why two people can look at the same evidence and reach opposite conclusions. They're measuring from different contexts. Their shadow priors are different."),
                    AwakeningSection(heading: "Shadow Work in Quantum Terms", body: "Now map this to everything we've covered:\n\nSHADOW = ENTANGLED STATES\nYour conscious traits and your shadow traits are entangled. They're not separate — they're complementary aspects of the same system. You can't fully describe one without the other.\n\nJung's Thinking/Feeling dichotomy? In quantum terms, these are non-commuting observables. You can't precisely measure both simultaneously. When Thinking dominates (engineering mode), Feeling goes into superposition (shadow). It's still there — it's just not being measured. And unmeasured states still influence the system.\n\nPROJECTION = CONTEXT-DEPENDENT COLLAPSE\nWhen you project your shadow onto someone else, you're performing a measurement in the wrong basis. You think you're measuring THEM, but your measurement apparatus (your perception) is calibrated to your own shadow. The outcome tells you more about your instrument than about the thing you're measuring.\n\nINTEGRATION = UPDATING THE MEASUREMENT BASIS\nShadow work doesn't eliminate the entangled states. It changes how you measure. You develop the ability to observe both Thinking AND Feeling without one collapsing the other. In quantum terms: you expand your measurement basis to include more of the Hilbert space.\n\nSYNCHRONICITY = HIGH-INFORMATION SURPRISE\nIn Friston's free-energy framework: your brain flags events that have very low probability in your current model but very high significance. That's a synchronicity. It's your brain's pattern detection system saying: \"This data point doesn't fit your model. Update required.\"\n\nNo quantum mysticism needed. Just the mathematics of how complex systems process information under uncertainty."),
